The Politics

The MSCS board’s 7–2 vote illustrates a structural problem that will recur wherever large technology projects enter communities with major unmet infrastructure needs.

A school district with more than a billion dollars in maintenance needs faces a choice between accepting a concrete offer today or continuing to wait for resources that may not arrive tomorrow. (https://t.co/7gQ8l2EEFv)

That is not a theoretical pressure.

It is the bargaining environment.

A community may reasonably conclude that a new gym, HVAC system or laboratory is better than no new gym, HVAC system or laboratory. But accepting an immediate benefit can also reduce the practical leverage available to demand something more comprehensive later.

That is why community-benefit agreements should be evaluated not simply by what was offered, but by:

• what was legally required;
• what was promised;
• what was actually contracted;
• what was delivered;
• what was delayed;
• what was renegotiated;
• and what remains outstanding.

The Memphis record provides enough information to make that comparison possible.

